1
00:00:20,507 --> 00:00:25,650
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is the Sutta on the Eight Thoughts of a Great Person.

2
00:00:26,770 --> 00:00:27,471
[SPEAKER_04]: What Sutta is that?

3
00:00:28,371 --> 00:00:31,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Anguttara Nikaya 8.30.

4
00:00:35,915 --> 00:00:43,159
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, on one occasion, Venerable Anuruddha, alone in seclusion, contemplated the following.

5
00:00:46,241 --> 00:00:49,162
[SPEAKER_01]: The following Eight Thoughts of a Great Person.

6
00:00:50,023 --> 00:00:50,303
[SPEAKER_04]: Seven.

7
00:00:51,205 --> 00:00:51,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Seven.

8
00:00:51,665 --> 00:00:52,725
[SPEAKER_01]: He contemplated only seven.

9
00:00:57,987 --> 00:01:01,248
[SPEAKER_01]: And number one is, this dhamma is for one...

10
00:01:01,328 --> 00:01:03,649
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just read all seven, and then we can discuss later.

11
00:01:04,429 --> 00:01:08,550
[SPEAKER_01]: This dhamma is for one who is not seeking admiration.

12
00:01:09,911 --> 00:01:11,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Not for one who is seeking admiration.

13
00:01:13,832 --> 00:01:17,413
[SPEAKER_01]: This dhamma is for one who is content, not for one who is discontent.

14
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:24,181
[SPEAKER_01]: This dhamma is for one who resorts to seclusion, not for one who delights in society.

15
00:01:25,342 --> 00:01:28,562
[SPEAKER_01]: This dhamma is for one who is diligent, not for one who is lazy.

16
00:01:30,223 --> 00:01:34,864
[SPEAKER_01]: This dhamma is for one who has a good memory, not who is forgetful.

17
00:01:35,824 --> 00:01:39,425
[SPEAKER_01]: This dhamma is for one who is settled, not for one who is unsettled.

18
00:01:40,245 --> 00:01:43,546
[SPEAKER_01]: This dhamma is for one who is wise, not for one who is foolish.

19
00:01:45,796 --> 00:01:59,642
[SPEAKER_01]: And as he was thinking these thoughts, the Blessed One knew what was in the mind of Venerable Aniruddha, and then said to him, it is commendable that you have reflected on these thoughts of a great person.

20
00:02:00,622 --> 00:02:05,845
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, Aniruddha, also reflect on this eighth thought of a great person.

21
00:02:06,705 --> 00:02:13,668
[SPEAKER_01]: This dhamma is for one who delights in non-proliferation, not for one who delights in proliferation.

22
00:02:16,134 --> 00:02:31,905
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just to add there, the Buddha says further that when you reflect on these eight thoughts, then you will be able to attain, as you wish, the jhānas, all four jhānas.

23
00:02:33,399 --> 00:02:49,747
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you do that, then, all of the requisites, basic requisites, you know, so, alms, just, you know, rough alms food, rough clothes, rough shelter, rough medicine, will feel like luxuries.

24
00:02:55,655 --> 00:03:07,538
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just keep that in mind, that these... thinking these thoughts leads to jhāna and pleasant abiding with basic requisites, the basic of the basic, the roughest of the rough.

25
00:03:10,899 --> 00:03:15,880
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, the Buddha goes on further and describes each of these points.

26
00:03:18,381 --> 00:03:21,902
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what was meant by the statement, this dhamma is for those

27
00:03:23,900 --> 00:03:41,592
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's usually translated as, this dhamma is for one who has no desires.

28
00:03:41,612 --> 00:03:42,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Few desires.

29
00:03:42,332 --> 00:03:42,953
[SPEAKER_00]: Few desires.

30
00:03:43,293 --> 00:03:45,294
[SPEAKER_01]: But the next point is one who is content.

31
00:03:45,795 --> 00:03:47,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Who is content with little.

32
00:03:47,936 --> 00:03:48,677
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like the same.

33
00:03:49,217 --> 00:03:51,899
[SPEAKER_01]: But the Pali word is, it means,

34
00:03:53,337 --> 00:03:54,598
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unassuming.

35
00:03:56,600 --> 00:04:01,564
[SPEAKER_01]: And, you could say, one who is not pretentious.

36
00:04:04,507 --> 00:04:16,998
[SPEAKER_01]: And the explanation that the Buddha gives, says here, when a bhikkhu is one who doesn't seek admiration, he does not wish, let other people know me to be one who doesn't seek admiration.

37
00:04:18,073 --> 00:04:22,100
[SPEAKER_01]: When he is content, he doesn't wish let other people know me as content.

38
00:04:22,280 --> 00:04:27,269
[SPEAKER_01]: When he's in solitude, he doesn't wish that people know me to be one who resorts to solitude.

39
00:04:27,910 --> 00:04:31,256
[SPEAKER_01]: When he's diligent, he does not wish let other people know me to be diligent.

40
00:04:32,200 --> 00:04:38,142
[SPEAKER_01]: When he is recollected, mindful, he doesn't wish, let other people know me to be recollected.

41
00:04:38,923 --> 00:04:41,804
[SPEAKER_01]: When he's settled, he doesn't wish for others to know him as settled.

42
00:04:42,404 --> 00:04:45,145
[SPEAKER_01]: When he is wise, he doesn't wish others to know him as wise.

43
00:04:45,345 --> 00:04:50,107
[SPEAKER_01]: And when he's non-proliferated, he doesn't wish others to know that he's non-proliferated.

44
00:04:52,488 --> 00:04:58,830
[SPEAKER_03]: So, he doesn't go around seeking admiration, desiring to be known.

45
00:05:03,442 --> 00:05:12,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's right, that's a bit too coarse.

46
00:05:13,308 --> 00:05:16,550
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, a bit too pretentious.

47
00:05:16,730 --> 00:05:28,658
[SPEAKER_04]: But, eh... you could see just to the point of not... not... it's not like, oh, I want to be not known, but it's certainly on the level of, well, I don't want to be known either.

48
00:05:29,618 --> 00:05:30,859
[SPEAKER_04]: Because sometimes people can think,

49
00:05:31,570 --> 00:05:38,115
[SPEAKER_04]: In order to be humble and unassuming and not seeking admiration, I must seek the opposite.

50
00:05:38,496 --> 00:05:39,477
[SPEAKER_04]: That's not the case either.

51
00:05:39,957 --> 00:05:50,726
[SPEAKER_04]: So, he simply practices out of his own wisdom, for his own benefit, whether others know him on account of it or not, it's irrelevant, it bears no relevance, it's not a reference point for him.

52
00:05:51,747 --> 00:05:58,512
[SPEAKER_04]: So, he doesn't wish to be known, that doesn't mean he will prevent to be known, if others do know him on their own accord.

53
00:05:59,995 --> 00:06:03,056
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, he would go out of his way to hide himself, or something like that.

54
00:06:03,076 --> 00:06:04,157
[SPEAKER_04]: That's not the case either.

55
00:06:04,517 --> 00:06:06,197
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you would do that, because you still care.

56
00:06:07,358 --> 00:06:08,398
[SPEAKER_03]: Now, you just go the other way.

57
00:06:10,299 --> 00:06:26,345
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's why it's the... it's the... it's like, obviously, the subtlest thread that goes from the most coarse pretentiousness, pompousness, admiration, to just

58
00:06:27,501 --> 00:06:33,383
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you have perception of others, a value in the perception of others knowing what you're doing?

59
00:06:35,504 --> 00:06:39,126
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, because there doesn't need to be any value in that.

60
00:06:39,306 --> 00:06:40,426
[SPEAKER_04]: It's relevant, in other words.

61
00:06:41,666 --> 00:06:50,110
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you're doing it, you're doing it for the sake of practice, for the sake of benefit, for the sake of, like, settlement and all the other things that follow.

62
00:06:50,290 --> 00:06:55,752
[SPEAKER_04]: So, but then, what if your mind still thinks about whether others know you or not?

63
00:06:57,038 --> 00:06:59,460
[SPEAKER_04]: It still values a degree of admiration.

64
00:06:59,560 --> 00:07:05,885
[SPEAKER_04]: How do you deal with that?

65
00:07:05,925 --> 00:07:06,205
[SPEAKER_03]: Isn't...

66
00:07:07,546 --> 00:07:18,515
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, the fundamental issue with that is... is the other... is... is... as long as there is this sense of self, there will be the other.

67
00:07:19,415 --> 00:07:21,036
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, yeah, that's why it is there.

68
00:07:21,176 --> 00:07:22,237
[SPEAKER_04]: But that's not the question.

69
00:07:22,257 --> 00:07:24,858
[SPEAKER_04]: The question is, how do you deal with that which is there?

70
00:07:24,958 --> 00:07:26,940
[SPEAKER_04]: How do you... By uprooting the sense of self would be...

71
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:29,321
[SPEAKER_04]: But how can you uproot it if you're still admiring others?

72
00:07:29,901 --> 00:07:31,342
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, you're seeking the admiration of others?

73
00:07:31,442 --> 00:07:31,542
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

74
00:07:31,562 --> 00:07:33,263
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that you start by seeking admiration.

75
00:07:34,044 --> 00:07:34,824
[SPEAKER_04]: How do you uproot it?

76
00:07:34,884 --> 00:07:38,006
[SPEAKER_04]: Because seeking admiration maintains that which you're trying to uproot.

77
00:07:38,847 --> 00:07:43,890
[SPEAKER_02]: So... By seeing that seeking and thereby not being

78
00:07:45,299 --> 00:07:53,187
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not a matter of choice, though, isn't it?

79
00:07:53,767 --> 00:07:57,851
[SPEAKER_04]: Not being pressured by it.

80
00:07:57,911 --> 00:07:58,892
[SPEAKER_04]: Not acting out of it.

81
00:07:58,912 --> 00:07:59,533
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the point.

82
00:07:59,633 --> 00:08:03,377
[SPEAKER_04]: So, seeking admiration, how is that done?

83
00:08:04,848 --> 00:08:07,431
[SPEAKER_04]: It's done by acting out of that wanting admiration.

84
00:08:07,551 --> 00:08:11,996
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the mind might still value others knowing this, but are you seeking it?

85
00:08:12,276 --> 00:08:13,317
[SPEAKER_04]: Are you acting out of it?

86
00:08:13,838 --> 00:08:15,239
[SPEAKER_04]: Are you making others known?

87
00:08:15,320 --> 00:08:17,822
[SPEAKER_04]: Are you hinting so that you are known as such and such?

88
00:08:18,403 --> 00:08:22,067
[SPEAKER_04]: All of those are intentional actions, efforts that you make.

89
00:08:22,107 --> 00:08:23,188
[SPEAKER_04]: So, don't do that.

90
00:08:24,390 --> 00:08:27,753
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, if the mind is still seeking admiration, well, that's fine.

91
00:08:27,793 --> 00:08:28,793
[SPEAKER_04]: You endure that part.

92
00:08:29,354 --> 00:08:30,295
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't act out of it.

93
00:08:30,315 --> 00:08:38,181
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't add fuel to it, in terms of your choices and actions that would then be contributing to that need for admiration.

94
00:08:38,701 --> 00:08:46,287
[SPEAKER_04]: And by not fueling it, and carrying on practicing, putting it aside, that which pressures you to act out of, it will subside.

95
00:08:47,088 --> 00:08:48,068
[SPEAKER_04]: You will stop valuing it.

96
00:08:48,809 --> 00:08:52,652
[SPEAKER_04]: You will then, one day, just find yourself, oh, I stop caring whether others know or not.

97
00:08:53,990 --> 00:08:59,711
[SPEAKER_04]: But you won't stop caring magically or on account of some sort of specific thing to do.

98
00:08:59,811 --> 00:09:11,393
[SPEAKER_04]: It's more like, no, you have to have endured that pressure for admiration, for recognition, sufficiently enough, and not having acted out of it, not feeling justified in acting out of it, not fueling it further.

99
00:09:12,273 --> 00:09:15,354
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, if others know you're not, if they admire you're not, that's not a problem.

100
00:09:16,554 --> 00:09:19,014
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, praise and blame, it's what moves the entire world.

101
00:09:19,375 --> 00:09:21,835
[SPEAKER_04]: It's one of the worldly winds, so it's a deeply rooted thing.

102
00:09:22,680 --> 00:09:25,701
[SPEAKER_04]: Being praised, being blamed is irrationally feared.

103
00:09:26,542 --> 00:09:28,283
[SPEAKER_04]: Being praised is irrationally liked.

104
00:09:29,403 --> 00:09:32,205
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's not just something you can choose to not be affected by.

105
00:09:32,985 --> 00:09:34,466
[SPEAKER_04]: But how do you go beyond it?

106
00:09:34,746 --> 00:09:35,606
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the question.

107
00:09:35,686 --> 00:09:37,987
[SPEAKER_04]: And the question... the answer to that is just as we said.

108
00:09:38,127 --> 00:09:39,768
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, first, don't act out of it.

109
00:09:40,248 --> 00:09:44,650
[SPEAKER_04]: So, same if you are blamed, well, don't act out of it immediately.

110
00:09:44,691 --> 00:09:47,332
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, don't go, well, let me explain myself.

111
00:09:47,432 --> 00:09:47,652
[SPEAKER_04]: Why?

112
00:09:48,032 --> 00:09:49,733
[SPEAKER_04]: Is that rooted in fear of being blamed?

113
00:09:50,817 --> 00:09:52,238
[SPEAKER_04]: Or, what difference does it make?

114
00:09:52,539 --> 00:09:55,541
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, even if say, well, I'm blamed wrongly.

115
00:09:56,042 --> 00:09:57,403
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, but did you do something wrong?

116
00:09:57,463 --> 00:09:57,703
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

117
00:09:57,763 --> 00:09:59,345
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, then you don't need to explain yourself.

118
00:09:59,365 --> 00:10:05,890
[SPEAKER_04]: The only reason you would be explaining yourself is because you don't like being blamed, regardless of whether it's righteous blame or not.

119
00:10:06,611 --> 00:10:07,831
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you're acting out of weakness.

120
00:10:07,871 --> 00:10:08,672
[SPEAKER_04]: You're not settled.

121
00:10:09,232 --> 00:10:10,012
[SPEAKER_04]: You're not wise.

122
00:10:10,132 --> 00:10:11,192
[SPEAKER_04]: You're fueling it further.

123
00:10:11,212 --> 00:10:16,033
[SPEAKER_04]: You're fueling your dependence on irrational winds of the world, how they move.

124
00:10:16,994 --> 00:10:17,234
[SPEAKER_04]: Same.

125
00:10:17,754 --> 00:10:20,614
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you relish the praise?

126
00:10:21,775 --> 00:10:22,695
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you incline towards it?

127
00:10:22,715 --> 00:10:23,295
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you welcome it?

128
00:10:23,315 --> 00:10:24,115
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you delight in it?

129
00:10:24,475 --> 00:10:26,196
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly like you would do to central thoughts.

130
00:10:28,356 --> 00:10:30,597
[SPEAKER_00]: I think part of that also is

131
00:10:33,419 --> 00:10:45,283
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, you know, when you're blamed, not necessarily by everybody, but certainly by certain people, you start kind of from the point of view of actually kind of

132
00:10:48,007 --> 00:10:54,911
[SPEAKER_00]: If I'm blamed by somebody whose, let's say, opinion I respect, that means I'm blameworthy.

133
00:10:55,131 --> 00:11:02,595
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you kind of almost define your own sense of right and wrong in terms of how others see you.

134
00:11:03,075 --> 00:11:03,215
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

135
00:11:03,235 --> 00:11:09,398
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the same, like, when you're praised, the reason people kind of... it's so kind of intoxicating is that that means I'm good.

136
00:11:09,919 --> 00:11:10,259
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

137
00:11:10,399 --> 00:11:12,280
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm objectively good.

138
00:11:13,300 --> 00:11:14,761
[SPEAKER_00]: Because others see me as good.

139
00:11:14,941 --> 00:11:16,342
[SPEAKER_04]: Because I've been praised, yeah.

140
00:11:16,622 --> 00:11:20,064
[SPEAKER_04]: So... Yeah, that's like... And the dhamma is not for that kind of person.

141
00:11:20,124 --> 00:11:22,865
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no, that needs to... You have to... You don't know what is good or bad.

142
00:11:23,125 --> 00:11:27,947
[SPEAKER_04]: You have to have endured that quite a lot, if you if you're still moved on that level.

143
00:11:28,628 --> 00:11:29,888
[SPEAKER_04]: Means you're not authentic enough.

144
00:11:30,349 --> 00:11:34,671
[SPEAKER_04]: So, but that is a practice already, yeah, enduring it on the right level.

145
00:11:35,351 --> 00:11:39,493
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, yes, as I said, if you're blamed, you look about four.

146
00:11:39,893 --> 00:11:40,653
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it factual?

147
00:11:42,014 --> 00:11:43,595
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it actions that have been rooted?

148
00:11:43,635 --> 00:11:46,056
[SPEAKER_04]: In which case, sure, you make your amends to that extent.

149
00:11:47,164 --> 00:11:57,148
[SPEAKER_04]: But, even then, you will pretty quickly recognize, if you are acting out of it, because, irrationally, you just resist the discomfort of blame.

150
00:11:57,508 --> 00:11:58,628
[SPEAKER_04]: Just... that doesn't matter.

151
00:11:58,708 --> 00:11:59,328
[SPEAKER_04]: Doesn't matter who.

152
00:11:59,829 --> 00:12:01,369
[SPEAKER_04]: Doesn't matter if somebody respects you or not.

153
00:12:02,109 --> 00:12:05,070
[SPEAKER_04]: Basically, if people blame you, that must be avoided.

154
00:12:05,851 --> 00:12:09,292
[SPEAKER_04]: Because... for no... for no other reason, simply because it's unpleasant.

155
00:12:10,157 --> 00:12:26,481
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's... for as long as you act out of those things, or try to avoid, or prevent, or kind of wiggle out, you will also then be cultivating the opposite of it, which is you will be gratuitously still valuing praise, for no reason, for no fact.

156
00:12:26,521 --> 00:12:32,362
[SPEAKER_04]: And if there is a factual reason, well, the facticity of that praise is what matters, not whether somebody praised you about it or not.

157
00:12:32,402 --> 00:12:33,082
[SPEAKER_04]: That's irrelevant.

158
00:12:33,925 --> 00:12:38,726
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if somebody praises you because you're so wise, well, it's your wisdom that matters there, not the fact that somebody praised you.

159
00:12:38,766 --> 00:12:42,388
[SPEAKER_04]: That did not make it more palpable or more valuable.

160
00:12:42,688 --> 00:12:44,348
[SPEAKER_04]: And you will start to differentiate it.

161
00:12:44,388 --> 00:12:48,629
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, whether they praise you or blame you, no, you know where the line for what's good and what's bad is.

162
00:12:49,910 --> 00:12:51,930
[SPEAKER_04]: What's blameworthy, you would see within yourself.

163
00:12:55,231 --> 00:12:57,152
[SPEAKER_01]: There's that... that's that danger of...

164
00:12:58,193 --> 00:13:04,076
[SPEAKER_01]: Praise, the big danger of praise, because it's so easily... it's intoxicated.

165
00:13:04,096 --> 00:13:04,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

166
00:13:04,556 --> 00:13:06,156
[SPEAKER_01]: From the start, you don't have any wisdom.

167
00:13:06,557 --> 00:13:06,777
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

168
00:13:07,117 --> 00:13:08,597
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you get praise from the start.

169
00:13:11,619 --> 00:13:16,841
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, if you're not authentic enough, yeah, it will be a very easy, eh... slippery, slippery slope.

170
00:13:16,861 --> 00:13:18,842
[SPEAKER_01]: And for monastics, you get ordained.

171
00:13:18,862 --> 00:13:19,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

172
00:13:19,222 --> 00:13:23,944
[SPEAKER_01]: You become praised as an arahant almost immediately in some, you know, some cultures.

173
00:13:24,024 --> 00:13:24,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

174
00:13:24,404 --> 00:13:25,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Everyone's bowing down to you, so...

175
00:13:27,253 --> 00:13:27,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Wow.

176
00:13:28,574 --> 00:13:31,437
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you're going to do the work when you're already... the whole world's...

177
00:13:31,457 --> 00:13:39,544
[SPEAKER_04]: But most importantly, really, you are praised if you, you know, toe the line of the established tradition, for example.

178
00:13:40,164 --> 00:13:43,307
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you're praised on account of external conformity.

179
00:13:44,685 --> 00:13:51,210
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that does... like, you know, whether somebody is elevated, but people bow to them or not, that's... you know, it's a bit coarse.

180
00:13:51,511 --> 00:13:53,952
[SPEAKER_04]: I think most people would see the line there.

181
00:13:53,973 --> 00:13:55,734
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, it's just a traditional thing, whatever.

182
00:13:55,794 --> 00:14:07,043
[SPEAKER_04]: But, where people would not see the line, is emulating the external form, appearance, tradition, etiquette, for accomplishment.

183
00:14:08,484 --> 00:14:13,028
[SPEAKER_04]: So, now, if I actually meet these requirements of external behavior, that is

184
00:14:13,727 --> 00:14:19,375
[SPEAKER_04]: That is what is good in and of itself, and that is praiseworthy in and of itself.

185
00:14:20,096 --> 00:14:27,546
[SPEAKER_04]: If I thus don't meet these equally kind of pointless, often pointless, requirements, oh, that is blameworthy.

186
00:14:27,987 --> 00:14:30,470
[SPEAKER_04]: So, basically, by developing that attitude,

187
00:14:31,431 --> 00:14:40,500
[SPEAKER_04]: You are completely obscuring the domain of intentions, which are the root factor of whether something is actually praiseworthy or blameworthy.

188
00:14:41,121 --> 00:14:48,348
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you have committed to such an external etiquette, norm, practice, tradition, observances, authorities,

189
00:14:49,207 --> 00:15:10,963
[SPEAKER_04]: It will result in you being absolutely convinced now that you are pure and praiseworthy and upholding the dhamma while you are actually even more... you have drifted even more further away from basic recognition of your intentionality behind everything you do, which you were probably closer to when you started practicing than you are now.

190
00:15:12,484 --> 00:15:15,006
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's not like how I do it and how it's... No.

191
00:15:15,226 --> 00:15:16,647
[SPEAKER_04]: Why am I doing it?

192
00:15:17,888 --> 00:15:20,172
[SPEAKER_04]: Greed, aversion or distraction.

193
00:15:20,593 --> 00:15:22,856
[SPEAKER_04]: Or non-greed, non-aversion, non-distraction.

194
00:15:23,377 --> 00:15:24,399
[SPEAKER_04]: That is the criteria.

195
00:15:24,920 --> 00:15:30,990
[SPEAKER_04]: But that's not something that can be measured as easily as a perfect mode of comportment.

196
00:15:32,043 --> 00:15:39,666
[SPEAKER_03]: There are people going, he speaks so softly, he never raises his voice, he moves his hands slowly, he whispers, he floats in the air.

197
00:15:40,206 --> 00:15:41,266
[SPEAKER_04]: That is praiseworthy.

198
00:15:41,846 --> 00:15:59,973
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, not according to the suttas, because the Buddha did, in a number of passages, mentioned there was that whole... if you might have a companion in a holy life that is softly spoken, friendly, praised by everybody, lovely to be with, amazing company, just so gentle in every way,

199
00:16:00,653 --> 00:16:01,694
[SPEAKER_04]: But of a wrong view.

200
00:16:01,854 --> 00:16:04,376
[SPEAKER_04]: And you should avoid him as you would avoid a charcoal pit.

201
00:16:05,197 --> 00:16:08,159
[SPEAKER_04]: Because he's just... No, that's not what you're practicing here.

202
00:16:08,800 --> 00:16:17,446
[SPEAKER_04]: Then you can have a holy companion who is rough, hard to listen to, hard to live with, difficult person, but is of a right view.

203
00:16:17,967 --> 00:16:22,090
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, you should then make the effort and endure that and live with him, because you can actually learn.

204
00:16:24,492 --> 00:16:25,413
[SPEAKER_04]: But if the entire

205
00:16:26,442 --> 00:16:38,133
[SPEAKER_04]: If all of us are only guided by the external norm and comportment and right branding of right words, then, yes, others will praise us on account of it, and then, if you don't meet their requirements, you'll be blamed on account of it.

206
00:16:38,213 --> 00:16:41,976
[SPEAKER_04]: But neither of those things really matter for where the practice is and where the intentions are.

207
00:16:43,197 --> 00:16:47,641
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's not like you need to now resist and reject all the conventions and so on.

208
00:16:48,423 --> 00:17:02,277
[SPEAKER_04]: That would be equally misguided, but you certainly need to start really recognizing what is the source of an action that makes it either praiseworthy or blameworthy, regardless of whether others praise you on account of it or blame you on account of it.

209
00:17:02,738 --> 00:17:09,565
[SPEAKER_04]: So, now you have many different styles, many different traditions, many different schools, many different branches of tradition, many different interpretations and all this.

210
00:17:09,945 --> 00:17:16,591
[SPEAKER_04]: In other words, things have proliferated, so there is no more a unified, even external, criteria.

211
00:17:16,711 --> 00:17:27,980
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, you can use common sense, but, as I said, people tend to subjugate the common sense to the... whether this is praised or blamed by the general population of spiritual practitioners.

212
00:17:28,121 --> 00:17:29,362
[SPEAKER_04]: If they like it, I shall do it.

213
00:17:29,402 --> 00:17:30,903
[SPEAKER_04]: If they don't like it, I shall not do it.

214
00:17:31,988 --> 00:17:34,629
[SPEAKER_04]: But that's only a relative criteria you should be considering.

215
00:17:34,689 --> 00:17:38,492
[SPEAKER_04]: Internally, it should be rooted in where is my intention behind this.

216
00:17:39,072 --> 00:17:40,853
[SPEAKER_04]: That's what makes it good, that's what makes it bad.

217
00:17:41,273 --> 00:17:48,117
[SPEAKER_04]: If you were to ask most people today, they would say, no, we think you should not be celibate and sense restraint.

218
00:17:48,177 --> 00:17:50,878
[SPEAKER_04]: That's too much, that's repression, that's bad for the mind.

219
00:17:51,218 --> 00:17:53,260
[SPEAKER_04]: You should practice your spirituality without that.

220
00:17:54,520 --> 00:17:57,842
[SPEAKER_04]: So, would you then just draw a line there, or...

221
00:17:57,862 --> 00:17:58,763
[SPEAKER_04]: So, where do you draw a line?

222
00:17:58,783 --> 00:17:59,483
[SPEAKER_04]: What is the line?

223
00:18:00,755 --> 00:18:09,426
[SPEAKER_04]: Or, if the tradition requires you to spend, you know, all the time in, like, massive groups of people, hundreds of monks, thousands of laities, for days, or just constantly...

224
00:18:09,446 --> 00:18:16,575
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, the Buddha told of sekas, almost arahants, for gathering together and talking over sewing, like, patching robes and stuff.

225
00:18:16,595 --> 00:18:18,397
[SPEAKER_04]: He said, this is like a fisherman's market.

226
00:18:19,778 --> 00:18:27,461
[SPEAKER_04]: But today it's deemed acceptable that thousands of us come together for days and just eat and talk and sit and... And bless.

227
00:18:27,681 --> 00:18:31,222
[SPEAKER_04]: And it's regarded as a holy, noble thing to do.

228
00:18:32,762 --> 00:18:34,803
[SPEAKER_04]: Because everybody likes it.

229
00:18:34,843 --> 00:18:36,304
[SPEAKER_04]: But is that the criteria?

230
00:18:37,364 --> 00:18:40,125
[SPEAKER_04]: If it's universally praised, is that why it is good?

231
00:18:40,905 --> 00:18:45,507
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you could find plenty of other things that are universally praised that you know you won't be doing.

232
00:18:48,468 --> 00:18:49,549
[SPEAKER_04]: So, and that's the whole point.

233
00:18:49,749 --> 00:19:05,079
[SPEAKER_04]: Once you start clarifying that thread between praise and blame and admiration and now... Well, if you follow that thread all the way as it takes you, you will abandon any and every conceit, internally or externally.

234
00:19:05,219 --> 00:19:07,560
[SPEAKER_04]: Any sense of self, internally or externally.

235
00:19:08,040 --> 00:19:14,925
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it wouldn't be that surprising or mystical or magical how you would then abide in jhāna at will.

236
00:19:16,863 --> 00:19:19,683
[SPEAKER_04]: Because it's not just mere reasoning.

237
00:19:19,703 --> 00:19:23,324
[SPEAKER_04]: Sure, you can reason, but then follow the same thread all the way.

238
00:19:24,724 --> 00:19:29,885
[SPEAKER_04]: And anything that partakes in the conceit, in the admiration, you don't act out of it.

239
00:19:30,285 --> 00:19:33,846
[SPEAKER_04]: Doesn't matter if the whole world says, no, no, you should do it, venerable sir, it is good.

240
00:19:34,646 --> 00:19:41,927
[SPEAKER_04]: You know that it's not, because you now see a direct reference point for whether it's good or not, which is your intention.

241
00:19:42,007 --> 00:19:46,228
[SPEAKER_04]: Intention for admiration, or intention that dismisses it and doesn't want anything to do with it.

242
00:19:47,045 --> 00:19:49,667
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not pressured, you're not acting under the pressure of the world.

243
00:19:50,107 --> 00:19:50,748
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

244
00:19:50,768 --> 00:20:02,536
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's the way to basically to reach this point, to start recognizing this subtle thread, is to, yeah, endure the pressure of praise, of tradition, of external requirements.

245
00:20:03,456 --> 00:20:10,541
[SPEAKER_04]: You know the precepts, you know the vinaya, you know the sense restraint, you know the suttas, you don't need any other authority, you don't need any other.

246
00:20:11,322 --> 00:20:16,085
[SPEAKER_04]: If you need other things to keep you together as a monk, you better start, you know,

247
00:20:18,058 --> 00:20:20,001
[SPEAKER_01]: The praise of others to keep you together.

248
00:20:20,361 --> 00:20:23,886
[SPEAKER_04]: Or, or the, or the, like, uniformity of the tradition or something.

249
00:20:24,767 --> 00:20:26,909
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, because without it, I just can't do it myself.

250
00:20:27,010 --> 00:20:28,912
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, means you can't practice yourself.

251
00:20:29,092 --> 00:20:30,895
[SPEAKER_04]: And practice is for oneself.

252
00:20:31,175 --> 00:20:35,060
[SPEAKER_04]: So, in other words, for as long as you're doing it, you won't be practicing it.

253
00:20:36,714 --> 00:20:50,476
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like that Sutta Majjhimaṇikāyeva 8, where it says, like, the way to self-effacement is, yeah, just to reflect, well, others may be cruel, because here I won't be cruel.

254
00:20:50,917 --> 00:20:57,218
[SPEAKER_00]: Others may have wrong, whatever, but here, like, whatever the world does, is irrelevant.

255
00:20:57,238 --> 00:20:57,338
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

256
00:20:57,798 --> 00:20:59,158
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm alone, actually.

257
00:20:59,318 --> 00:21:02,719
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm alone, and I'm taking that on, and that's where the practice is.

258
00:21:02,879 --> 00:21:04,779
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is how you would then, yeah...

259
00:21:05,559 --> 00:21:06,800
[SPEAKER_01]: It kind of ties into that other one.

260
00:21:07,621 --> 00:21:11,863
[SPEAKER_01]: He delights in solitude, not in society.

261
00:21:11,963 --> 00:21:12,484
[SPEAKER_04]: In company.

262
00:21:12,664 --> 00:21:13,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

263
00:21:13,384 --> 00:21:15,866
[SPEAKER_01]: In the ways of society, in the views of society.

264
00:21:15,986 --> 00:21:16,266
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

265
00:21:18,007 --> 00:21:34,178
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, and there's that other sutta where it's said that one... like the... about the wrong reasons to go forth, like one goes forth and then gains renown and fame, and he's contented with that, and that's where he stops, and that's not why one should go forth.

266
00:21:36,155 --> 00:21:37,476
[SPEAKER_01]: It just reminds me of another sutta.

267
00:21:37,496 --> 00:21:39,577
[SPEAKER_01]: If we can just turn that one for...

268
00:21:41,999 --> 00:21:43,600
[SPEAKER_01]: It's Dīgha Nikāya 1.

269
00:21:45,261 --> 00:21:46,701
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, eh...

270
00:21:47,062 --> 00:21:55,667
[SPEAKER_01]: If bhikkhus, others, speak in dispraise of me, or in dispraise of a dhamma or the saṅgha, you should not give way to resentment, displeasure or animosity against them.

271
00:21:56,027 --> 00:22:01,991
[SPEAKER_01]: For if you were to become angry or upset in such a situation, you would only be creating an obstacle for yourself.

272
00:22:02,571 --> 00:22:05,373
[SPEAKER_01]: If you become angry or upset when others speak in dispraise,

273
00:22:07,960 --> 00:22:12,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Certainly not, Lord.

274
00:22:12,366 --> 00:22:22,441
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and if others speak in this praise of me, the dhamma or the saṅgha, you should unravel what is false and point out

275
00:22:23,872 --> 00:22:24,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is false saying.

276
00:22:24,953 --> 00:22:27,155
[SPEAKER_01]: For such and such a reason, this is wrong.

277
00:22:27,695 --> 00:22:28,335
[SPEAKER_01]: This is untrue.

278
00:22:28,415 --> 00:22:29,596
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no such thing in this.

279
00:22:29,676 --> 00:22:31,017
[SPEAKER_01]: This is not found among us.

280
00:22:31,938 --> 00:22:38,523
[SPEAKER_01]: And if others speak in praise of me, the dhamma or the saṅgha, you should not give way to elation, joy and happiness.

281
00:22:38,563 --> 00:22:44,748
[SPEAKER_01]: For if you were to become excited in such a way, you would only be creating an obstacle for yourself.

282
00:22:45,368 --> 00:22:49,572
[SPEAKER_01]: If others speak in praise of me, the dhamma or the saṅgha, you should acknowledge what is in fact

283
00:22:50,287 --> 00:22:50,547
[SPEAKER_01]: True.

284
00:22:51,427 --> 00:22:53,748
[SPEAKER_01]: For such and such a reason, this is a fact.

285
00:22:53,848 --> 00:22:54,448
[SPEAKER_01]: This is true.

286
00:22:55,088 --> 00:22:56,388
[SPEAKER_01]: There is such a thing in us.

287
00:22:56,448 --> 00:22:57,369
[SPEAKER_01]: This is found among us.

288
00:22:59,149 --> 00:23:11,592
[SPEAKER_01]: And he goes on further to say, it is only in regard to insignificant matters and minor details of mere moral virtue that a worldly and ordinary person would refer when speaking in praise of the Buddha.

289
00:23:13,332 --> 00:23:13,852
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he wouldn't...

290
00:23:14,032 --> 00:23:15,853
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't be able to see them all.

291
00:23:15,893 --> 00:23:17,453
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no capacity they developed.

292
00:23:17,473 --> 00:23:17,653
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

293
00:23:18,718 --> 00:23:37,607
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, but the bottom line is, see, either way, you don't make it personal by acting out of your own personality, so to speak, or you are... you are related, or you are upset, so now you're referring, addressing, dealing with the other person that said that, or did that.

294
00:23:37,767 --> 00:23:37,887
No.

295
00:23:38,407 --> 00:23:45,210
[SPEAKER_04]: Just point out what is factually wrong view as a wrong view, or what is the right aspect of what they said as a right aspect.

296
00:23:46,383 --> 00:23:49,205
[SPEAKER_04]: It doesn't need to go... it doesn't need to become personal either way.

297
00:23:49,325 --> 00:23:50,665
[SPEAKER_04]: It doesn't need to go further than that.

298
00:23:52,026 --> 00:23:56,549
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, I am this, you are... What was said is factually wrong because of this, this and this.

299
00:23:56,809 --> 00:24:00,171
[SPEAKER_04]: And, yeah, this was... that was correct because of this, this and this.

300
00:24:00,311 --> 00:24:05,514
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't really care who said it, why they said it and, you know, what type of person, what kind of person they are and so on.

301
00:24:05,634 --> 00:24:06,214
[SPEAKER_04]: It's irrelevant.

302
00:24:07,455 --> 00:24:10,516
[SPEAKER_04]: If it is relevant, it's because you have taken it personally.

303
00:24:10,997 --> 00:24:13,218
[SPEAKER_04]: And if you take it personally, does that mean

304
00:24:14,862 --> 00:24:20,305
[SPEAKER_04]: Can you take things personally and have perspective at the same time on things that you take in person?

305
00:24:20,345 --> 00:24:20,506
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

306
00:24:20,626 --> 00:24:24,968
[SPEAKER_04]: But taking things personally means automatically losing a degree of perspective.

307
00:24:25,389 --> 00:24:30,172
[SPEAKER_04]: And then you will not be able to see whether they're factually, objectively right or wrong in what they're saying.

308
00:24:32,933 --> 00:24:34,114
[SPEAKER_04]: So that's why it's an obstacle.

309
00:24:34,815 --> 00:24:38,237
[SPEAKER_04]: You are creating an obstruction for yourself unnecessarily.

310
00:24:38,277 --> 00:24:39,898
[SPEAKER_04]: Because there's no reason to take it personally.

311
00:24:39,918 --> 00:24:40,859
[SPEAKER_04]: You just did it out of that

312
00:24:41,914 --> 00:24:46,455
[SPEAKER_04]: Unchecked habits, acting out of it and not wanting to endure the discomfort of that pressure.

313
00:24:47,736 --> 00:24:49,256
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, but he was attacking me.

314
00:24:49,336 --> 00:24:50,336
[SPEAKER_04]: He was talking about me.

315
00:24:50,516 --> 00:24:53,417
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, and even imagine the worst-case scenario.

316
00:24:53,437 --> 00:24:54,017
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, he was.

317
00:24:54,637 --> 00:24:56,958
[SPEAKER_04]: He is disrespecting you, nobody else.

318
00:24:57,838 --> 00:24:59,319
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is that your problem?

319
00:25:01,239 --> 00:25:03,020
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, I don't quite know.

320
00:25:03,060 --> 00:25:04,200
[SPEAKER_04]: It just feels uncomfortable.

321
00:25:04,480 --> 00:25:07,781
[SPEAKER_04]: I just take that painful feeling for granted and I want to get rid of it.

322
00:25:09,624 --> 00:25:10,485
[SPEAKER_04]: So, how'd you get rid of it?

323
00:25:10,505 --> 00:25:12,346
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, by defending my honor, or something.

324
00:25:13,246 --> 00:25:25,295
[SPEAKER_04]: Or by not taking yourself, so... Yeah, well, that's one way to try to get rid of it, but have you freed yourself from it in the past, whenever you defended your honor, and justified yourself, and showed everybody that they were wrong, and you were right?

325
00:25:26,475 --> 00:25:28,657
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, no, I'm still here, equally affected.

326
00:25:29,097 --> 00:25:34,821
[SPEAKER_04]: And again, that's why the Buddha did compare enduring the words of others to just putting up and bearing the elements.

327
00:25:37,752 --> 00:25:39,944
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, it's going to be felt uncomfortably.

328
00:25:40,758 --> 00:25:44,621
[SPEAKER_04]: Because of your personality, conceit is still there, degrees of it.

329
00:25:45,282 --> 00:25:47,584
[SPEAKER_04]: Not because, you know, it's a problem in and of itself.

330
00:25:47,924 --> 00:25:59,594
[SPEAKER_04]: But if... and if someone was blaming you for something, you could actually... Well, by not acting personally, by not taking personally, then you would actually be able to see, impersonally, whether the blame is factually correct, maybe.

331
00:25:59,634 --> 00:26:01,215
[SPEAKER_04]: Maybe you have been doing something wrong.

332
00:26:01,595 --> 00:26:03,977
[SPEAKER_04]: And now you have a chance to... And now you see it, yeah.

333
00:26:04,338 --> 00:26:06,519
[SPEAKER_03]: And you still don't need to take it personally.

334
00:26:06,740 --> 00:26:09,502
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why you would be able to see this and actually rectify it.

335
00:26:11,815 --> 00:26:18,178
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's making what is right and what is wrong more important than yourself.

336
00:26:18,478 --> 00:26:22,920
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, and everybody, that's the thing with that, everybody kind of already knows that.

337
00:26:24,040 --> 00:26:26,101
[SPEAKER_04]: Even the complete Asuttava Putujana.

338
00:26:26,922 --> 00:26:36,506
[SPEAKER_04]: Say, somebody wants to sort out their life, you know, maybe draw a line for people that are taking advantage of them, maybe draw a line for themselves.

339
00:26:37,066 --> 00:26:38,087
[SPEAKER_04]: If you were to ask them,

340
00:26:38,913 --> 00:26:42,915
[SPEAKER_04]: In that, like a non-imperative sense, what do you think you should do?

341
00:26:43,855 --> 00:26:49,778
[SPEAKER_04]: They would probably, 99% of them, would know exactly what they need to do to make their life better and their mind calmer and more settled.

342
00:26:50,719 --> 00:26:51,699
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, they won't be able to do it.

343
00:26:53,040 --> 00:26:58,383
[SPEAKER_04]: Because they will still put their personality first, which then, it would also then require...

344
00:26:58,423 --> 00:27:02,505
[SPEAKER_04]: It would be hard to uphold these determinations, these decisions, these knowledges.

345
00:27:03,337 --> 00:27:05,338
[SPEAKER_04]: Of what you need to do, because it's just...

346
00:27:05,498 --> 00:27:11,821
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm just so used to acting out of the pressure of senses, sensuality, personality, and the views.

347
00:27:12,402 --> 00:27:13,842
[SPEAKER_04]: This is just too abstract.

348
00:27:14,162 --> 00:27:17,704
[SPEAKER_04]: Although, factually, I know what I need to do to make my life better.

349
00:27:17,804 --> 00:27:21,606
[SPEAKER_04]: I know where the line is, where I should draw it, where I should say no to people, and so on.

350
00:27:22,987 --> 00:27:27,029
[SPEAKER_04]: But I just can't.

351
00:27:27,089 --> 00:27:31,091
[SPEAKER_04]: I was like, well, that's why... Like, if you can't do even that, then you won't.

352
00:27:31,131 --> 00:27:32,932
[SPEAKER_04]: You know, the dhamma simply doesn't apply to you.

353
00:27:34,240 --> 00:27:38,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Just another sutta which might correspond to this.

354
00:27:40,143 --> 00:27:46,887
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the four types of people with no integrity and people with integrity.

355
00:27:48,948 --> 00:27:52,830
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the dhamma is not for one with no integrity, you could say.

356
00:27:54,211 --> 00:28:00,374
[SPEAKER_01]: And this sutta says, a person of no integrity, when unasked,

357
00:28:01,324 --> 00:28:04,546
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, he speaks about himself, one way or the other.

358
00:28:04,566 --> 00:28:06,647
[SPEAKER_01]: He speaks about himself.

359
00:28:06,707 --> 00:28:09,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, let me teach you the dhamma by speaking about myself.

360
00:28:31,942 --> 00:28:34,584
[SPEAKER_01]: As a child, I was like this... Well, whatever.

361
00:28:34,684 --> 00:28:39,147
[SPEAKER_04]: Whichever way, shape and form that can take, you could see where the emphasis is.

362
00:28:39,287 --> 00:28:50,756
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it on the points made, freedom from contradiction, clarity of instruction, or is it on, I've been through these experiences, I've done this, I've seen this, I've...

363
00:28:51,276 --> 00:28:52,097
[SPEAKER_04]: Completely unnecessary.

364
00:28:52,377 --> 00:28:53,458
[SPEAKER_04]: Even if it were factual.

365
00:28:54,159 --> 00:28:56,941
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, if it were factual, you wouldn't be emphasizing yourself.

366
00:28:57,341 --> 00:28:58,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, it's... As simple as that.

367
00:29:00,014 --> 00:29:10,464
[SPEAKER_01]: The other side of it says, he is one who speaks of his own, if he's asked and pressed, he speaks of his own good points, not in full, not in detail, with omissions, holding back.

368
00:29:11,665 --> 00:29:18,251
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if he's pressed, even if he's pressed, so if he's asked, okay, then he holds back a bit.

369
00:29:22,594 --> 00:29:25,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, obviously, it's all about yourself.

370
00:29:26,695 --> 00:29:27,456
[SPEAKER_01]: We're coming across.

371
00:29:27,996 --> 00:29:32,240
[SPEAKER_01]: For you, you want other people to know you as someone great.

372
00:29:35,243 --> 00:29:37,765
[SPEAKER_01]: To partake in your love for yourself, basically.

373
00:29:37,945 --> 00:29:40,648
[SPEAKER_01]: I want others to love me as much as I love myself.

374
00:29:43,551 --> 00:29:45,833
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the second point.

375
00:29:45,853 --> 00:29:49,376
[SPEAKER_01]: This dhamma is for one who is content, not for one who is discontent.

376
00:29:51,198 --> 00:29:51,298
[SPEAKER_02]: And

377
00:29:54,066 --> 00:30:00,931
[SPEAKER_01]: This means that he's content with any kind of requisites, robes, elm fruit, lodgings and medicine.

378
00:30:01,471 --> 00:30:02,792
[SPEAKER_04]: And you can take that same principle.

379
00:30:02,932 --> 00:30:05,173
[SPEAKER_04]: So, again, take that thread as far as it goes.

380
00:30:05,634 --> 00:30:07,375
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I content or am I discontent?

381
00:30:08,796 --> 00:30:10,637
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what is the... what is discontentment?

382
00:30:12,338 --> 00:30:13,659
[SPEAKER_03]: When is a person discontent?

383
00:30:14,660 --> 00:30:16,241
[SPEAKER_00]: When they want something that they don't have.

384
00:30:16,461 --> 00:30:17,442
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, exactly, yeah.

385
00:30:17,882 --> 00:30:21,725
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's not like necessarily... you're not discontent by...

386
00:30:22,571 --> 00:30:28,595
[SPEAKER_04]: By being in a bad mood, for example, you are responsible for your discontent.

387
00:30:28,716 --> 00:30:30,817
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, sure, you can be in a bad mood on account of that.

388
00:30:31,918 --> 00:30:48,630
[SPEAKER_04]: But the discontent is, yeah, so, I am cultivating, welcoming, delighting, and so on, acting out of the attitude of, oh, it shouldn't be this way, I want it set another way, or I don't want it now, or I didn't have enough of it.

389
00:30:48,650 --> 00:30:51,652
[SPEAKER_04]: So, a certain degree of entitlement, righteousness,

390
00:30:52,733 --> 00:30:55,815
[SPEAKER_04]: All that would make you discontent.

391
00:30:56,015 --> 00:30:57,676
[SPEAKER_04]: Wanting basically what you don't have.

392
00:30:58,497 --> 00:30:59,858
[SPEAKER_04]: Wanting more of what you have.

393
00:30:59,878 --> 00:31:03,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Wanting the external world to be a certain way.

394
00:31:04,061 --> 00:31:10,345
[SPEAKER_04]: Just whenever there is a discontent, you will find it in one of these ways of your going about it.

395
00:31:10,385 --> 00:31:12,126
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you are responsible for it directly.

396
00:31:12,146 --> 00:31:19,531
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why if you then abandon that and cultivate the contentment with whichever way your situation is,

397
00:31:21,856 --> 00:31:30,419
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, you will actually follow, again, that thread as far as it goes, and it would result in kind of, pretty much, completely uprooting sensuality.

398
00:31:31,799 --> 00:31:38,841
[SPEAKER_04]: If you follow the thread of contentment with any condition of the body, any requisites, any access.

399
00:31:38,901 --> 00:31:41,142
[SPEAKER_04]: No sense of removal of the slightest sense of entitlement.

400
00:31:41,582 --> 00:31:45,923
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why, I think, discontent was one of the Mara's daughters.

401
00:31:47,784 --> 00:31:51,305
[SPEAKER_04]: Discontent sensuality goes hand in hand.

402
00:31:52,630 --> 00:31:53,891
[SPEAKER_04]: It's rooted in desires.

403
00:31:55,953 --> 00:32:03,961
[SPEAKER_00]: It's interesting, like, because it's kind of obvious that contentment, like, which is more pleasant?

404
00:32:04,001 --> 00:32:05,943
[SPEAKER_00]: Contentment or discontent?

405
00:32:05,963 --> 00:32:06,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

406
00:32:06,443 --> 00:32:14,511
[SPEAKER_00]: It's obvious that contentment is more pleasant, but it's not the kind of pleasure already that's on the level of the kind of pleasure that people

407
00:32:15,093 --> 00:32:18,056
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's exactly the principle of sensuality.

408
00:32:18,496 --> 00:32:24,162
[SPEAKER_04]: In and of itself, the prospect of sensuality is actually unpleasant.

409
00:32:24,522 --> 00:32:28,606
[SPEAKER_04]: To realize, I would be more contented if I don't act out of this.

410
00:32:28,626 --> 00:32:29,607
[SPEAKER_04]: This leaves me alone.

411
00:32:30,308 --> 00:32:36,614
[SPEAKER_04]: If I don't make this worse, that is more contenting for myself.

412
00:32:37,503 --> 00:32:44,788
[SPEAKER_04]: But, that already requires recognition and wisdom and discernment of, well, certainly of the nature of sensuality to a great degree.

413
00:32:45,568 --> 00:32:50,892
[SPEAKER_04]: Because otherwise, your assumptions are on the level, no, no, contentment comes after I get what I want.

414
00:32:54,027 --> 00:32:59,853
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the only purpose of that wanting is more wanting, if you look at it from this perspective?

415
00:32:59,873 --> 00:32:59,973
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

416
00:33:00,633 --> 00:33:02,735
[SPEAKER_04]: But from the individual point of view...

417
00:33:02,755 --> 00:33:03,316
[SPEAKER_04]: I can't see that.

418
00:33:03,756 --> 00:33:17,650
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, you won't be seeing that, so then the only purpose of... well, basically, the only idea of contentment is the contentment on account of sensuality that you know, which means anything less than your desires being met will be experienced as discontent.

419
00:33:18,270 --> 00:33:18,891
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you're seeing

420
00:33:20,358 --> 00:33:29,048
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, it's tricking you, this sensuality, into seeing discontent as content, as something which is just delightful.

421
00:33:29,528 --> 00:33:31,570
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, it is a perversion of perception, yeah.

422
00:33:31,931 --> 00:33:36,035
[SPEAKER_04]: That which is actually discontent, you're perceiving as content.

423
00:33:36,756 --> 00:33:40,180
[SPEAKER_04]: That which is actually painful, you're perceiving as pleasant, as agreeable.

424
00:33:44,095 --> 00:34:03,094
[SPEAKER_02]: Whereas, if you were to see it, then you would just endure this already given desire, which is painful, and therefore, yeah, be able to maintain this perspective and not fuel it, and that's the only way for it to... And then, even the pressure of it would eventually...

425
00:34:03,775 --> 00:34:04,215
[SPEAKER_04]: Disappear.

426
00:34:04,416 --> 00:34:04,616
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

427
00:34:05,036 --> 00:34:06,757
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, you wouldn't need to even endure anything.

428
00:34:06,777 --> 00:34:06,998
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

429
00:34:07,738 --> 00:34:08,219
[SPEAKER_00]: Be satisfied.

430
00:34:08,239 --> 00:34:09,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, already be content.

431
00:34:09,380 --> 00:34:09,940
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, exactly.

432
00:34:10,400 --> 00:34:10,601
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

433
00:34:10,681 --> 00:34:11,461
[SPEAKER_04]: The wound's healed.

434
00:34:12,102 --> 00:34:14,023
[SPEAKER_04]: No matter what touches it, it doesn't hurt anymore.

435
00:34:14,043 --> 00:34:14,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

436
00:34:14,344 --> 00:34:16,265
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no external thinking give you that.

437
00:34:16,445 --> 00:34:16,585
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

438
00:34:16,926 --> 00:34:17,086
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

439
00:34:17,466 --> 00:34:18,127
[SPEAKER_01]: No more prod.

440
00:34:18,647 --> 00:34:22,070
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, the best meal with just the amount, the perfect amount of salt and whatever.

441
00:34:22,090 --> 00:34:22,250
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

442
00:34:22,310 --> 00:34:23,511
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not seen there anymore.

443
00:34:23,671 --> 00:34:23,831
No.

444
00:34:25,573 --> 00:34:26,894
[SPEAKER_04]: Becomes irrelevant, basically.

445
00:34:28,624 --> 00:34:32,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether people praise you or blame you, doesn't matter.

446
00:34:32,925 --> 00:34:34,306
[SPEAKER_01]: And the third point.

447
00:34:35,886 --> 00:34:40,188
[SPEAKER_01]: This dhamma is for one who resorts to solitude, not for one who delights in society.

448
00:34:41,988 --> 00:34:43,489
[SPEAKER_01]: And what does that mean?

449
00:34:44,269 --> 00:34:57,413
[SPEAKER_01]: When a bhikkhu resorts to solitude, other bhikkhus, bhikkhunis, male lay followers, female lay followers, kings, royal ministers, heads of other religions and disciples belonging to other religions approach him.

450
00:34:58,376 --> 00:35:00,359
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he doesn't get involved... only so much, so that they...

451
00:35:18,487 --> 00:35:24,892
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, they answer what they came for, but they... you don't make them stay longer, you know, chatting them up further, and so on.

452
00:35:25,233 --> 00:35:27,975
[SPEAKER_04]: And that would come out of a mind that inclines towards solitude.

453
00:35:28,555 --> 00:35:37,823
[SPEAKER_04]: You can try and measure and learn how to speak, but fundamentally, are you delighting in company or are you content being alone?

454
00:35:37,863 --> 00:35:43,508
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, see, all of these thoughts, it's on the level of something you are cultivating or not.

455
00:35:44,290 --> 00:35:49,632
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why, if you cultivate them rightly, wisdom will be the result of it, and jhānas will be obtainable at will.

456
00:35:50,632 --> 00:35:55,173
[SPEAKER_04]: So, is your mind inclining to a solitude or to its others?

457
00:35:55,813 --> 00:35:59,114
[SPEAKER_04]: Spending time with others, talking with others, seeing others, dwelling with others?

458
00:35:59,154 --> 00:35:59,314
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

459
00:36:00,335 --> 00:36:02,435
[SPEAKER_04]: And, sure, you will not start.

460
00:36:02,555 --> 00:36:03,976
[SPEAKER_04]: Nobody will start suddenly.

461
00:36:04,036 --> 00:36:05,156
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, yeah, I prefer solitude.

462
00:36:08,083 --> 00:36:10,223
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's why it takes effort and discernment.

463
00:36:10,504 --> 00:36:21,386
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, yes, you then have to endure the pressure of wanting company, of acting towards company, of talking when it's not necessary, of somebody... well, they came to see me.

464
00:36:21,406 --> 00:36:27,367
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, okay, but have you made them stay longer through your incessant sort of, you know, speech and so on?

465
00:36:28,667 --> 00:36:31,168
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you could still see that it's on you.

466
00:36:32,684 --> 00:36:40,471
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, you are fueling the mind to incline towards society, towards company, or you will be removing that fuel, so the mind will start inclining towards solitude.

467
00:36:42,353 --> 00:36:57,286
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, again, you need to recognize that that's where the line is, because you can then have also people who just really hate others, are full of ill will, don't want to have to deal with them, so then they quote passages in solitude.

468
00:36:58,607 --> 00:37:02,589
[SPEAKER_04]: Because... not because the really mind inclines towards solitude.

469
00:37:03,369 --> 00:37:05,870
[SPEAKER_04]: Mind still inclines towards people just with ill-will.

470
00:37:07,611 --> 00:37:11,373
[SPEAKER_04]: So, in order to not experience the discomfort of ill-will, you just want to be left alone.

471
00:37:11,953 --> 00:37:23,378
[SPEAKER_04]: That's sometimes understandable, but it can also become like a view of others bother me so much, just sights of others, perceptions of others.

472
00:37:23,398 --> 00:37:24,879
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't want to have to deal with it.

473
00:37:26,179 --> 00:37:28,180
[SPEAKER_04]: And now that's a bit too much the other way.

474
00:37:28,996 --> 00:37:30,277
[SPEAKER_04]: Because it's rooted in aversion.

475
00:37:30,898 --> 00:37:33,019
[SPEAKER_04]: So, does that mean you go and seek the company?

476
00:37:33,039 --> 00:37:33,259
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

477
00:37:34,400 --> 00:37:38,324
[SPEAKER_04]: That means when the company is there, well, is your mind filled with ill-will?

478
00:37:38,704 --> 00:37:40,806
[SPEAKER_01]: You're still intimately engaged with it.

479
00:37:40,826 --> 00:37:41,366
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, exactly.

480
00:37:41,406 --> 00:37:41,807
[SPEAKER_04]: That's what I mean.

481
00:37:41,827 --> 00:37:44,889
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, so, one is sort of overly affirming it, the other is trying to deny it.

482
00:37:44,909 --> 00:37:47,171
[SPEAKER_04]: Either way, it's still defined by the company.

483
00:37:48,512 --> 00:37:52,836
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you don't need to then seek the company to prove to yourself that you don't hate the company.

484
00:37:53,556 --> 00:37:57,680
[SPEAKER_04]: But, when the company is inevitably present, which will have to happen,

485
00:37:58,418 --> 00:38:03,064
[SPEAKER_04]: You see, is my mind now inclining towards solitude out of this neutral sort of... No.

486
00:38:03,224 --> 00:38:06,148
[SPEAKER_04]: Solitude is beneficial for the development of the mind and everything else.

487
00:38:06,628 --> 00:38:08,150
[SPEAKER_04]: Or is it because I just don't want to deal with this?

488
00:38:08,531 --> 00:38:09,332
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't want to deal with this.

489
00:38:09,372 --> 00:38:10,293
[SPEAKER_04]: I don't want to talk to others.

490
00:38:10,333 --> 00:38:11,815
[SPEAKER_04]: I just... Is it ill will?

491
00:38:13,076 --> 00:38:14,979
[SPEAKER_04]: If it's so, well, that's your problem now.

492
00:38:15,159 --> 00:38:17,602
[SPEAKER_04]: It's a different type of problem, but it's nevertheless a problem.

493
00:38:18,960 --> 00:38:22,761
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't help yourself but getting involved, even just mentally.

494
00:38:22,921 --> 00:38:23,161
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

495
00:38:23,242 --> 00:38:29,023
[SPEAKER_04]: So, sure, you can carry on avoiding the company, but now you have to do it for the right reasons.

496
00:38:29,144 --> 00:38:30,964
[SPEAKER_04]: Don't think that your work is completed.

497
00:38:31,844 --> 00:38:41,388
[SPEAKER_04]: Having avoided company on account of ill-will, or not wanting to be bothered, don't think your work is completed simply by avoiding company, because now you have to deal with the ill-will and being bothered.

498
00:38:42,108 --> 00:38:43,788
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yes, avoid the company and then deal with this.

499
00:38:44,449 --> 00:38:48,230
[SPEAKER_04]: The next time company is there, you will have neither ill-will nor delight in them.

500
00:38:51,860 --> 00:39:07,017
[SPEAKER_00]: In case it's relevant, the Āgama, parallel to that Sutta, and at this point where it defines seclusion, it says, it's somewhat shorter, it just says, he resorts to two types of seclusion, physical seclusion and mental seclusion.

501
00:39:08,178 --> 00:39:18,688
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, it's kind of interesting in relation to what you just said, because, like, even if somebody is in physical seclusion, on account of others... Yeah, it's exactly that.

502
00:39:18,868 --> 00:39:20,770
[SPEAKER_04]: You're not mentally secluded.

503
00:39:20,830 --> 00:39:22,292
[SPEAKER_04]: You're still dwelling in that company.

504
00:39:25,054 --> 00:39:25,875
[SPEAKER_04]: What parallel is that?

505
00:39:26,636 --> 00:39:28,978
[SPEAKER_00]: To this, to the... To this citta.

506
00:39:29,158 --> 00:39:30,659
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't remember precisely which number.

507
00:39:37,599 --> 00:39:38,560
[SPEAKER_01]: And point number four.

508
00:39:38,900 --> 00:39:43,043
[SPEAKER_01]: The dhamma is for one who is diligent, not for one who is lazy.

509
00:39:44,684 --> 00:39:49,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Here Bhikkhu is resolved on abandoning unwholesome qualities and acquiring wholesome qualities.

510
00:39:49,828 --> 00:39:56,092
[SPEAKER_01]: He is strong, firm in exertion, not casting off the duty of cultivating wholesome qualities.

511
00:40:00,695 --> 00:40:01,476
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's...

512
00:40:01,496 --> 00:40:03,097
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, we covered that in so many other talks.

513
00:40:03,237 --> 00:40:04,038
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, it's pretty clear.

514
00:40:04,866 --> 00:40:08,247
[SPEAKER_04]: How you abandon and what you endure and... Not a procrastinator.

515
00:40:09,508 --> 00:40:10,148
[SPEAKER_01]: Not procrastinating.

516
00:40:10,168 --> 00:40:14,629
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, and also, like, again, not misperceiving where your duty is, really.

517
00:40:14,689 --> 00:40:26,973
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, yes, I'm so diligent, you know, I do the chores and I fold my robe perfectly and I wash the bowl meticulously with, like, almost methodical meditation motions and so on, but that's not your duty.

518
00:40:27,994 --> 00:40:29,874
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you also tolerate the mind of sensuality?

519
00:40:30,295 --> 00:40:34,236
[SPEAKER_04]: Or do you make the effort to establish the context in regards to sensual thoughts that are besetting you?

520
00:40:34,982 --> 00:40:36,143
[SPEAKER_04]: Do you tolerate the mind of your will?

521
00:40:36,904 --> 00:40:47,433
[SPEAKER_04]: Or do you also make the effort that whenever it presents itself, even a possibility of it, you also then establish the mind, the perceptions of how this is not for your benefit, it is for your harm, and do you then endure that?

522
00:40:48,534 --> 00:40:49,935
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's where the diligence is.

523
00:40:52,234 --> 00:40:53,935
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I'd rather just deal with...

524
00:40:53,955 --> 00:40:57,997
[SPEAKER_04]: I'd rather just sweep the leaves and, you know, polish the floor and my duty is complete.

525
00:40:58,057 --> 00:40:59,818
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, if you're an arahant, you can just do that.

526
00:41:00,318 --> 00:41:02,939
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, yes, you would have then done all the boring that matters.

527
00:41:03,039 --> 00:41:04,980
[SPEAKER_04]: But if you're not, you are just deluding yourself.

528
00:41:06,392 --> 00:41:31,355
[SPEAKER_00]: That's, sorry to say, that there's this, yeah, short verse that just says, not for, not through feeble efforts, not through, like, lack of exertion, is this extinguishment to be attained, release from all suffering, but I find it, like, the the sutta that is going with that, is that same sutta where it,

529
00:41:32,176 --> 00:41:37,998
[SPEAKER_00]: He talks about this monk who is not doing the duties that are expected.

530
00:41:38,558 --> 00:41:42,960
[SPEAKER_00]: The other monks see that he's not participating as much in, like, the sewing of the robes and everything.

531
00:41:43,000 --> 00:41:44,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he was practicing jhāna, basically.

532
00:41:44,561 --> 00:41:45,541
[SPEAKER_00]: He was practicing jhāna.

533
00:41:45,581 --> 00:41:46,762
[SPEAKER_00]: He was doing his own duty.

534
00:41:46,922 --> 00:41:50,263
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's then the exertion that is obviously referred to as, like,

535
00:41:50,803 --> 00:42:02,736
[SPEAKER_00]: The other monks who were kind of so busy with everything else, they were the kind of ones more, yeah, of being... well, not necessarily, but yeah, you can't... you don't measure it by that, obviously.

536
00:42:05,479 --> 00:42:05,859
[SPEAKER_04]: But same.

537
00:42:06,820 --> 00:42:11,085
[SPEAKER_04]: Somebody can say, okay, so see, seclusion is better, I shouldn't do any duties again.

538
00:42:11,365 --> 00:42:12,907
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, start with that and then...

539
00:42:13,957 --> 00:42:15,438
[SPEAKER_04]: See why the state of mind is behind it?

540
00:42:15,818 --> 00:42:16,918
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it because you are lazy?

541
00:42:17,638 --> 00:42:20,219
[SPEAKER_04]: You can't even do the menial tasks?

542
00:42:20,779 --> 00:42:27,762
[SPEAKER_04]: How do you think you're going to be dealing with hindrances, if even sweeping the leaves is too disturbing for your mind?

543
00:42:27,842 --> 00:42:28,902
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, it's too much work.

544
00:42:29,943 --> 00:42:31,703
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, pfff, okay then.

545
00:42:31,723 --> 00:42:36,505
[SPEAKER_04]: You've tipped the whole thing upside down and you're refusing to even try to recalibrate it.

546
00:42:40,466 --> 00:42:42,067
[SPEAKER_01]: So, number five.

547
00:42:42,945 --> 00:42:49,892
[SPEAKER_01]: This dhamma is for one who is recollected or firm in mindfulness, good memory.

548
00:42:50,613 --> 00:42:51,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Not for one who is forgetful.

549
00:42:53,276 --> 00:42:53,957
[SPEAKER_01]: And what does that mean?

550
00:42:54,577 --> 00:42:59,983
[SPEAKER_01]: A bhikkhu is careful, prudent and has an excellent memory of what was said and done long ago.

551
00:43:00,886 --> 00:43:05,007
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, just in case it's relevant, the Agama defines that one slightly differently.

552
00:43:05,027 --> 00:43:10,868
[SPEAKER_00]: It just says he dwells in the four establishments of mindfulness, of recollectedness.

553
00:43:11,268 --> 00:43:13,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he's a sati, he's firmly established.

554
00:43:14,069 --> 00:43:14,269
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

555
00:43:14,989 --> 00:43:30,533
[SPEAKER_04]: So, how is your memory, how is your memory, if you combine the two, how would you remember what was said and done long time ago, of relevance, by remembering your posture?

556
00:43:33,027 --> 00:43:38,370
[SPEAKER_04]: Can I just be mindful of my knees and somehow the knowledge of what I heard a long time ago will just come back?

557
00:43:39,571 --> 00:43:40,712
[SPEAKER_04]: How would you remember anything?

558
00:43:41,732 --> 00:43:42,873
[SPEAKER_04]: Where did you find your memory?

559
00:43:43,554 --> 00:43:48,337
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it in physical sensations of your body, in the present moment, right here, right here, in the moment, right here?

560
00:43:49,057 --> 00:43:50,078
[SPEAKER_04]: Is that how you remember stuff?

561
00:43:51,338 --> 00:43:52,439
[SPEAKER_04]: No, we spoke about it before.

562
00:43:52,479 --> 00:44:01,625
[SPEAKER_04]: You remember by stepping away, by sort of... or at least temporarily stepping aside from absorption in sense objects.

563
00:44:03,624 --> 00:44:16,929
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why one who is practicing virtue, sense restraint, leading himself away from sense objects, from engagement with the content, is naturally becoming more mindful.

564
00:44:17,669 --> 00:44:19,550
[SPEAKER_04]: He's naturally becoming less forgetful.

565
00:44:19,950 --> 00:44:20,670
[SPEAKER_02]: Unknowledgeable.

566
00:44:21,451 --> 00:44:22,391
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, exactly.

567
00:44:22,791 --> 00:44:30,674
[SPEAKER_04]: Now that you're not forgetful, now that you are mindful, that you can recollect, exactly, now you have these contexts that are becoming more and more known.

568
00:44:32,497 --> 00:44:37,180
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's the four foundations of mindfulness, basically.

569
00:44:37,500 --> 00:44:47,466
[SPEAKER_04]: The four practices of surmounting the engagement with the content of your experience and discerning the nature of it.

570
00:44:48,166 --> 00:44:52,489
[SPEAKER_02]: If you engage with the content of your experience, you're not aware that you're engaged.

571
00:44:52,509 --> 00:44:53,769
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's already lost.

572
00:44:53,789 --> 00:44:57,812
[SPEAKER_04]: That's already a degree of lacking perspective.

573
00:45:00,530 --> 00:45:22,814
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you would then take that thread all the way, as we said before, thread of recollectedness, well, there will be, in the end, impossible for you to not have perspective upon your situation as a whole, of anatta, anicca, everything else that you need to understand on account of dhamma, that you need to discern in these five aggregates.

574
00:45:23,654 --> 00:45:29,155
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the only way to discern the five aggregates correctly is to start withdrawing yourself from the content of your senses.

575
00:45:31,942 --> 00:45:33,302
[SPEAKER_04]: Because that's not where you're going to find them.

576
00:45:36,003 --> 00:45:43,885
[SPEAKER_03]: Also, the five hindrances, abandoning them, is directly related to remembering things that were said and done long ago.

577
00:45:44,485 --> 00:45:52,487
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, because, you know, if we're speaking about the non... the things that are present in a non-specific manner,

578
00:45:53,467 --> 00:45:59,712
[SPEAKER_04]: In a manner that you cannot measure as an object of your senses, a degree of presence, the five hindrances are the chief amongst them.

579
00:46:00,472 --> 00:46:04,836
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why, if you were to surmount them on that level, the mind will be in jhāna.

580
00:46:09,299 --> 00:46:16,044
[SPEAKER_02]: Whereas, the specific, again, is what is taking you away from the non-specific.

581
00:46:16,164 --> 00:46:16,905
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, you start.

582
00:46:17,005 --> 00:46:18,906
[SPEAKER_04]: You start by being taken away.

583
00:46:19,187 --> 00:46:19,507
[SPEAKER_04]: So then,

584
00:46:19,887 --> 00:46:24,092
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why virtue has to come... precepts have to come first.

585
00:46:24,172 --> 00:46:31,721
[SPEAKER_04]: That you don't even know to what extent, but by keeping the precepts and guarding of the sense doors, you are sort of

586
00:46:32,617 --> 00:46:46,862
[SPEAKER_04]: Withdrawing yourself gradually out of that overly proliferated specificity of your senses and pleasures and avoiding pain and so on, which is all down the rabbit holes of objects, sense objects, sensual proliferation.

587
00:46:47,042 --> 00:46:54,785
[SPEAKER_04]: Fundamentally, you're in the content, not because you haven't really collected all the information about how this comes about.

588
00:46:54,925 --> 00:46:57,326
[SPEAKER_04]: No, it's because you're not withdrawn from it sufficiently enough.

589
00:46:57,686 --> 00:46:57,866
[SPEAKER_04]: So,

590
00:46:58,626 --> 00:47:11,194
[SPEAKER_04]: Mentally, eh... have you endured the pull of the senses without acting out of it sufficiently enough, to the point that you're settled, not bothered by it, regardless of which way it takes, regardless of what object presents itself?

591
00:47:11,234 --> 00:47:15,137
[SPEAKER_04]: That's how you then dwell on the nature of the experience, which is what dhamma is, the nature.

592
00:47:16,348 --> 00:47:18,769
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's not a mystical way to access this nature.

593
00:47:18,809 --> 00:47:38,374
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, no, withdrawing yourself from the content, starting from sensuality, acting out of will-will, thoughts of cruelty, eventually up to the five hindrances, and then, yeah, you will be the dhamma, you will be on the level of discerning the nature of the experience, because you have actually made that diligent effort to withdraw yourself from everything else that was obstructing it.

594
00:47:38,814 --> 00:47:40,994
[SPEAKER_04]: It's basically what you attempt, or how you attempt.

595
00:47:41,394 --> 00:47:42,335
[SPEAKER_04]: It's yonisonmissical.

596
00:47:42,755 --> 00:47:43,895
[SPEAKER_04]: It's both, to begin with.

597
00:47:44,933 --> 00:47:49,436
[SPEAKER_04]: Because a person can think, okay, I can attend to sensuality as long as I attend it rightly.

598
00:47:49,477 --> 00:47:52,299
[SPEAKER_04]: In the sense of, I am already pulled by sensuality.

599
00:47:52,439 --> 00:47:52,699
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no.

600
00:47:52,739 --> 00:47:55,421
[SPEAKER_04]: Certain things, you have to avoid even certain what's.

601
00:47:56,782 --> 00:48:07,050
[SPEAKER_04]: Until eventually, of course, you have fully overcome the wrong type of attention, then you would not be able to attend it in that old way that you thought it was a neutral way from which I can practice mindfulness.

602
00:48:07,070 --> 00:48:07,210
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

603
00:48:07,250 --> 00:48:13,075
[SPEAKER_04]: Certain things need to be stopped and abandoned and sensuality, ill will, must not be welcomed and so on.

604
00:48:14,029 --> 00:48:22,312
[SPEAKER_04]: But, fundamentally, you want to uproot it, then, yeah, you have to endure those states of mind, not having acted out of them.

605
00:48:22,592 --> 00:48:29,174
[SPEAKER_04]: So, when I say you've got to deny this sensuality, it means you've got to deny your engagement with it, because that is what sensuality is for you.

606
00:48:31,235 --> 00:48:38,217
[SPEAKER_04]: And then endure the, as we said before, the discontent of the unfulfilled desire,

607
00:48:39,355 --> 00:48:43,659
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you realize, okay, so that's one thing, but me acting on account of it, that's another thing.

608
00:48:43,699 --> 00:48:50,784
[SPEAKER_04]: So I should first stop acting on account of it, not being moved by it, become settled in regard to it, and then you stop being bothered by it.

609
00:48:51,985 --> 00:48:52,826
[SPEAKER_01]: And point number six.

610
00:48:53,867 --> 00:48:57,090
[SPEAKER_01]: The dhamma is for one who is settled, not for one who is unsettled.

611
00:48:57,670 --> 00:48:58,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Samahita.

612
00:49:01,313 --> 00:49:02,274
[SPEAKER_01]: And what does that mean?

613
00:49:02,994 --> 00:49:05,997
[SPEAKER_01]: It means one who is able to

614
00:49:07,604 --> 00:49:09,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Attain the jhānas, practice jhāna.

615
00:49:09,485 --> 00:49:14,869
[SPEAKER_03]: One who is able to be unmoved, unmoved by things that would otherwise move you.

616
00:49:14,889 --> 00:49:18,591
[SPEAKER_04]: So, and that's again, brings me back frequently to that point.

617
00:49:18,811 --> 00:49:27,157
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the whole samādhi thing, the whole practice of overcoming unwholesome, is not about getting rid of these unwholesome things and being unmoved by them.

618
00:49:27,517 --> 00:49:28,718
[SPEAKER_04]: That's how you get rid of them.

619
00:49:30,599 --> 00:49:32,961
[SPEAKER_04]: When you are unmoved by them, they'll stop coming.

620
00:49:33,701 --> 00:49:36,483
[SPEAKER_04]: Because they keep coming, because there's still possibility they could move you.

621
00:49:38,407 --> 00:49:39,848
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, point number seven.

622
00:49:40,508 --> 00:49:43,270
[SPEAKER_01]: This dhamma is for one who is wise, not for one who is foolish.

623
00:49:43,890 --> 00:49:45,571
[SPEAKER_04]: One who discerns, not one who doesn't.

624
00:49:46,031 --> 00:49:48,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, where... yeah, pañña, one with pañña.

625
00:49:49,033 --> 00:49:49,573
[SPEAKER_04]: Discernment.

626
00:49:49,833 --> 00:49:50,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

627
00:49:50,694 --> 00:49:58,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Here, wherever one goes, he is endowed with discernment that is noble and insightful, leading to the destruction of suffering.

628
00:49:58,978 --> 00:49:59,658
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's all.

629
00:50:00,359 --> 00:50:01,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Wherever you go, you understand that.

630
00:50:01,879 --> 00:50:03,120
[SPEAKER_04]: This is the context that he needs to see.

631
00:50:04,121 --> 00:50:05,401
[SPEAKER_04]: He protects it, he maintains it.

632
00:50:07,590 --> 00:50:09,211
[SPEAKER_00]: It seems to be referring to the right view.

633
00:50:10,372 --> 00:50:13,595
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, from the start to the end, yeah.

634
00:50:15,156 --> 00:50:19,660
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the dhamma is one for the right, who has the right, who has the right view, kind of.

635
00:50:21,561 --> 00:50:23,763
[SPEAKER_01]: And point number eight is kind of similar, actually.

636
00:50:25,324 --> 00:50:32,450
[SPEAKER_01]: This dhamma is for one who delights in non-proliferation, not for one who delights in proliferation.

637
00:50:34,704 --> 00:50:40,325
[SPEAKER_01]: He's one whose mind inclines towards the ending of proliferation, becomes calm, settles down, and is liberated.

638
00:50:42,025 --> 00:50:53,687
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's kind of saying the same, you know, you're not proliferating those obstacles, or you're not proliferating greed, acting out of greed, your anger, and your delusion.

639
00:50:54,247 --> 00:50:54,447
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

640
00:50:55,328 --> 00:51:01,129
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what is the... like, what is the basis for any proliferation?

641
00:51:01,149 --> 00:51:03,189
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you could proliferate thoughts and dhamma,

642
00:51:04,183 --> 00:51:14,988
[SPEAKER_04]: Not that that would be the dhamma for you at the time, but you could proliferate your sutta study, you could proliferate your Pali study, your scholarship, you could proliferate all these things.

643
00:51:15,028 --> 00:51:24,353
[SPEAKER_04]: You could proliferate just seeking answers to the questions that are relevant to the suffering and so on, but yet you're proliferating them.

644
00:51:24,413 --> 00:51:24,893
[SPEAKER_04]: Why is that?

645
00:51:25,673 --> 00:51:33,797
[SPEAKER_04]: What would be the difference between somebody not proliferating their thoughts on dhamma and another person proliferating their thoughts on dhamma?

646
00:51:34,575 --> 00:51:35,155
[SPEAKER_03]: What would be the...

647
00:51:36,976 --> 00:51:38,176
[SPEAKER_03]: Doing it with a sense of self.

648
00:51:39,397 --> 00:51:39,677
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay.

649
00:51:40,217 --> 00:51:43,258
[SPEAKER_04]: More specifically, both are doing it with a sense of self, because they're not arahants.

650
00:51:43,779 --> 00:51:45,119
[SPEAKER_04]: Ayoniso, manasikāra.

651
00:51:45,579 --> 00:51:47,360
[SPEAKER_04]: Ayoniso would be the necessary basis.

652
00:51:47,580 --> 00:51:50,961
[SPEAKER_04]: And what does ayoniso always result in?

653
00:51:51,042 --> 00:51:54,743
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, you mistake... you emphasize content rather than context.

654
00:51:54,803 --> 00:51:56,924
[SPEAKER_04]: You... And when would you do that?

655
00:51:57,004 --> 00:51:58,144
[SPEAKER_04]: When you put what first?

656
00:51:59,885 --> 00:52:00,925
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, yourself, basically.

657
00:52:00,985 --> 00:52:01,346
[SPEAKER_04]: But...

658
00:52:01,386 --> 00:52:02,366
[SPEAKER_04]: When you act out of what?

659
00:52:04,395 --> 00:52:15,921
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you start with craving, as we said before, but the point comes when you, through starting with craving, you learn that the craving is the root of suffering.

660
00:52:16,601 --> 00:52:27,066
[SPEAKER_04]: So now, you're thinking the dhamma, you're clarifying the ideas, you're studying Pali, but, really, you need to start asking yourself, is this rooted in craving?

661
00:52:28,587 --> 00:52:33,489
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I have doubt present in my mind about this sutta or that sutta,

662
00:52:34,909 --> 00:52:35,569
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it pleasant?

663
00:52:35,669 --> 00:52:36,390
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it unpleasant?

664
00:52:36,450 --> 00:52:37,150
[SPEAKER_04]: Or is it neutral?

665
00:52:37,310 --> 00:52:39,211
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I moved by the doubt first?

666
00:52:40,451 --> 00:52:40,651
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

667
00:52:41,812 --> 00:52:50,035
[SPEAKER_04]: If I am moved by the doubt, whatever I do, including, you know, answering these questions and getting the answer, will be rooted in the hindrance of doubt.

668
00:52:52,096 --> 00:53:01,380
[SPEAKER_04]: And you might do it once, you do it twice, you do it a hundred times, but if you have any wisdom or discernment, you'll start recognizing, okay, I have provided an answer to this,

669
00:53:02,302 --> 00:53:04,924
[SPEAKER_04]: I count these numbers of times from all sorts of directions.

670
00:53:06,105 --> 00:53:11,069
[SPEAKER_04]: How about now, I stop that, and I endure the pain of the doubt, and I don't act out of it.

671
00:53:11,549 --> 00:53:13,170
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, but then everything will fall apart.

672
00:53:13,210 --> 00:53:23,178
[SPEAKER_04]: And it's like, well, even if everything falls apart, I still know what I will not do, which is break the precepts, lose the sense restraint, you know, start wagging company.

673
00:53:24,359 --> 00:53:26,160
[SPEAKER_04]: I'll just endure everything falling apart.

674
00:53:27,648 --> 00:53:31,651
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, what's the definition of everything falling apart or nothing falling apart?

675
00:53:31,771 --> 00:53:33,893
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, things being there on my own terms?

676
00:53:34,653 --> 00:53:36,154
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's why they will fall apart.

677
00:53:36,995 --> 00:53:42,699
[SPEAKER_04]: Not because there's something wrong with them or even with my information, but because I want to hold them the way I think they should be held.

678
00:53:43,780 --> 00:53:45,562
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, then they should really fall apart.

679
00:53:46,202 --> 00:53:50,145
[SPEAKER_04]: And they will start falling apart if you start enduring the pressure of doubt and not acting out of it.

680
00:53:50,465 --> 00:53:52,647
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's how the first three factors will fall apart.

681
00:53:59,855 --> 00:54:00,556
[SPEAKER_04]: So, craving.

682
00:54:03,918 --> 00:54:10,382
[SPEAKER_04]: Craving led you to this, but then craving also made you understand and collect information that craving is the problem.

683
00:54:10,483 --> 00:54:20,690
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you can now carry on perpetuating that repetition of the same information for the rest of your life and studying etymology and get introduced to all the other ancient languages, you know, get a degree in all of it.

684
00:54:21,617 --> 00:54:26,419
[SPEAKER_04]: But fundamentally, what you learn in the very beginning, that craving is a problem, you haven't dealt with it.

685
00:54:26,540 --> 00:54:29,681
[SPEAKER_03]: You still haven't dealt with that craving, because you're still fueling it.

686
00:54:29,701 --> 00:54:40,166
[SPEAKER_04]: Sure, you're not fueling it in the ways of sensuality, in the ways of mindless asceticism, but you are very much fueling it in the ways of views.

687
00:54:40,487 --> 00:54:42,888
[SPEAKER_04]: So, either way, you are bound by it.

688
00:54:43,998 --> 00:54:46,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you're delighting in distracting yourself.

689
00:54:46,560 --> 00:54:46,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

690
00:54:47,240 --> 00:54:48,822
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not looking at that context.

691
00:54:48,842 --> 00:54:51,723
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, and that's a... there is a delight in proliferation.

692
00:54:53,064 --> 00:55:01,110
[SPEAKER_01]: And the word is papāñca, which can be like an obstacle, so... proliferating obstacles.

693
00:55:01,890 --> 00:55:02,210
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

694
00:55:06,453 --> 00:55:11,877
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just, I mean, a silly kind of question, but why would now... why would I be in jhāna?

695
00:55:13,437 --> 00:55:16,620
[SPEAKER_01]: By thinking these thoughts...

696
00:55:16,800 --> 00:55:18,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Someone might ask...

697
00:55:18,141 --> 00:55:18,921
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how does I get...

698
00:55:19,041 --> 00:55:25,646
[SPEAKER_04]: So, if you think these thoughts rightly, follow the thread all the way, establish your mind upon it... Act accordingly.

699
00:55:26,447 --> 00:55:26,687
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

700
00:55:26,867 --> 00:55:27,908
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, yeah, yeah.

701
00:55:27,948 --> 00:55:28,909
[SPEAKER_04]: That's presupposed.

702
00:55:29,009 --> 00:55:29,209
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

703
00:55:29,870 --> 00:55:32,331
[SPEAKER_04]: So, why would then that be the jhāna?

704
00:55:34,393 --> 00:55:42,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I think if you're contemplating it rightly, you're not just doing a study, figuring out the pilot words, but actually...

705
00:55:42,880 --> 00:55:45,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Looking into the meaning of it, you are seeing it there, you are knowing it.

706
00:55:46,642 --> 00:55:52,044
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, so why would knowing these things, still, why would knowing these things result in jhāna?

707
00:55:52,584 --> 00:55:53,425
[SPEAKER_04]: It will, pretty much.

708
00:55:54,405 --> 00:55:54,585
[SPEAKER_04]: Why?

709
00:55:55,946 --> 00:55:57,747
[SPEAKER_01]: What is jhāna?

710
00:55:58,087 --> 00:55:58,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Withdrawal.

711
00:55:59,087 --> 00:55:59,548
[SPEAKER_01]: From what?

712
00:56:00,248 --> 00:56:01,028
[SPEAKER_01]: From the external.

713
00:56:01,448 --> 00:56:03,569
[SPEAKER_00]: From sensuality, from unwholesome states.

714
00:56:04,070 --> 00:56:05,971
[SPEAKER_04]: From five hindrances, from unwholesome states.

715
00:56:06,051 --> 00:56:08,812
[SPEAKER_04]: So, by following these eight types of

716
00:56:09,798 --> 00:56:22,849
[SPEAKER_04]: Thoughts, for the ones that discern, you are fully, completely, surmounting, establishing the unshakable context in regard to anything that's unwholesome, anything that can even partake in the unwholesome.

717
00:56:23,790 --> 00:56:31,656
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, jhāna is, like, at will available, because you are perpetually outside of the domain of unwholesome.

718
00:56:31,756 --> 00:56:32,497
[SPEAKER_04]: Through discernment.

719
00:56:33,208 --> 00:56:40,394
[SPEAKER_04]: Through discernment, through enduring rightly, through discerning, through not acting out, through cultivating the eight thoughts, following the thread all the way.

720
00:56:41,215 --> 00:56:53,485
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you are not judging whether things are suitable or unsuitable, wholesome or unwholesome, by, as we said, what everybody agrees upon, what majority says, what the most senior monk in the world tells you.

721
00:56:53,805 --> 00:56:55,666
[SPEAKER_04]: No, you're judging it by whether it's rooted in

722
00:56:56,570 --> 00:56:57,731
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I seeking admiration?

723
00:56:58,372 --> 00:57:00,093
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I seeking sensuality?

724
00:57:00,153 --> 00:57:01,414
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I acting out of doubt?

725
00:57:01,675 --> 00:57:03,056
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I cultivating hindrances?

726
00:57:03,596 --> 00:57:07,760
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I proliferating my own craving through acting out of it while I know really I should do?

727
00:57:08,601 --> 00:57:10,042
[SPEAKER_04]: That becomes your criteria.

728
00:57:10,542 --> 00:57:12,905
[SPEAKER_04]: And am I diligent in regard to this criteria?

729
00:57:13,745 --> 00:57:14,346
[SPEAKER_04]: Am I lazy?

730
00:57:15,307 --> 00:57:17,509
[SPEAKER_04]: Or am I putting in the work on this level?

731
00:57:18,710 --> 00:57:20,431
[SPEAKER_04]: How can jhāna then not be the result?

732
00:57:20,571 --> 00:57:21,112
[SPEAKER_04]: Impossible.

